And the complexity and the
scale of enterprise customers.

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:00.550
You're seeing here a
visual flow builder.

00:12:00.550 --> 00:12:03.760
This is one of the big
asks that customers have.

00:12:03.760 --> 00:12:07.400
How do we create complex
flows with Dialogflow?

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:09.520
Dialogflow CX enables that.

00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:12.100
You're going to have a drag
and drop visual flow builder

00:12:12.100 --> 00:12:15.550
with graphical transition
that you can just

00:12:15.550 --> 00:12:18.050
set up and make sure that
you can build bots very,

00:12:18.050 --> 00:12:19.150
very quickly.

00:12:19.150 --> 00:12:22.640
It has native support for all
the things that Dialogflow has.

00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:26.800
The 20,010 per agent [INAUDIBLE]
agent, the native support

00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:30.580
for sentiment analysis at
each term, the custom voice

00:12:30.580 --> 00:12:32.350
support, and I'll
give you an example

00:12:32.350 --> 00:12:35.840
of custom voice with text to
speech that's really exciting.

00:12:35.840 --> 00:12:38.740
We have CI city pipeline
in Dialogflow CX, which

00:12:38.740 --> 00:12:40.810
allows you to do
A/B testing of bots

00:12:40.810 --> 00:12:42.730
and be able to have
two different versions

00:12:42.730 --> 00:12:45.730
of the same intent and be
able to test their performance

00:12:45.730 --> 00:12:47.660
before you deploy.

00:12:47.660 --> 00:12:52.100
We have created an advanced
NLU for Dialogflow CX, which

00:12:52.100 --> 00:12:55.340
is based on a massive
transformer report model

00:12:55.340 --> 00:12:59.220
and outperforms the
previous NLU model we had,

00:12:59.220 --> 00:13:02.150
which was already a
leader in its market.

00:13:02.150 --> 00:13:04.430
We have better analytics
and debugging tool,

00:13:04.430 --> 00:13:06.440
which are important
in large deployments

00:13:06.440 --> 00:13:08.400
because many people are
working on those bots.

00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:10.250
It's not just one
person, and you

00:13:10.250 --> 00:13:13.430
need to have those debugging
tools and analytics tools.

00:13:13.430 --> 00:13:16.460
And very importantly,
we heard of customers

00:13:16.460 --> 00:13:19.000
asking for simpler pricing.

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:21.530
The pricing on Dialogflow
CX is based on sessions.

00:13:21.530 --> 00:13:25.270
There's two prices, a voice
session and a chat session,

00:13:25.270 --> 00:13:26.310
and that's it.

00:13:26.310 --> 00:13:29.450
There's no consumption, no
time, no how many queries

00:13:29.450 --> 00:13:30.297
you're doing.

00:13:30.297 --> 00:13:32.130
You have a voice session,
there's one price.

00:13:32.130 --> 00:13:34.850
You have a chat session,
there's another price.

00:13:34.850 --> 00:13:37.580
Makes it very easy to
know how much you're

00:13:37.580 --> 00:13:39.690
going to spend
with Dialogflow CX,

00:13:39.690 --> 00:13:41.280
and we're really,
really exciting.

00:13:41.280 --> 00:13:43.670
So Dialogflow CX is
going to be beta--

00:13:43.670 --> 00:13:47.300
is beta today, sorry, and
we'll go GA in the next couple

00:13:47.300 --> 00:13:48.620
months, or few months.

00:13:51.250 --> 00:13:53.230
Let's focus now on Agent Assist.

00:13:53.230 --> 00:13:56.050
Agent Assist is all about
empowering human agents,

00:13:56.050 --> 00:13:58.600
making sure that human
agents are the most effective

00:13:58.600 --> 00:14:00.440
in their job.

00:14:00.440 --> 00:14:04.550
As we know, human agents
are oftentimes new.

00:14:04.550 --> 00:14:06.000
Maybe don't know
the new products.

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:08.570
Maybe are not comfortable
with some of the information

00:14:08.570 --> 00:14:10.820
that the customers
are asking them.

00:14:10.820 --> 00:14:14.260
So what it does, it does a live
transcription of all calls.

00:14:14.260 --> 00:14:17.930
And I'm saying all calls
in your contact center.

00:14:17.930 --> 00:14:20.420
Any call coming
through is transcribed.

00:14:20.420 --> 00:14:22.460
This is very new to a
lot of contact center.

00:14:22.460 --> 00:14:25.752
Most contact center-- actually,
many do call recording,

00:14:25.752 --> 00:14:27.710
but do they ever look at
those call recordings?

00:14:27.710 --> 00:14:29.390
Do they know what's
in their call?

00:14:29.390 --> 00:14:30.710
Not so much.

00:14:30.710 --> 00:14:32.480
With a live
transcription, you now

00:14:32.480 --> 00:14:34.790
have transcription
of every single call

00:14:34.790 --> 00:14:36.900
coming in your contact center.

00:14:36.900 --> 00:14:39.770
And those calls are used
and this transcription

00:14:39.770 --> 00:14:42.840
are used to do article
suggestion and FAQ

00:14:42.840 --> 00:14:45.370
suggestion in real time.

00:14:45.370 --> 00:14:47.720
It will listen to
the conversation,

00:14:47.720 --> 00:14:50.600
give you information,
documents, FAQs,

00:14:50.600 --> 00:14:55.410
whatever the agent needs to
answer a question will come in.

00:14:55.410 --> 00:14:59.180
We can also provide guidance
through a Dialogflow bot.

00:14:59.180 --> 00:15:03.590
If throughout a conversation
an intent mapping to a bot

00:15:03.590 --> 00:15:05.810
is detected, saying,
I want to book a trip,

00:15:05.810 --> 00:15:08.030
a bot can probably handle that.

00:15:08.030 --> 00:15:11.830
You need a destination, a date,
how many people, et cetera,

00:15:11.830 --> 00:15:12.460
et cetera.

00:15:12.460 --> 00:15:14.180
The bot can guide
the human agent

00:15:14.180 --> 00:15:15.680
through all those
questions and make

00:15:15.680 --> 00:15:19.100
sure the human agent
is not missing any.

00:15:19.100 --> 00:15:21.990
This is really helping the
agent be more efficient.

00:15:21.990 --> 00:15:24.140
Less time spent on the phone.

00:15:24.140 --> 00:15:27.800
More focused because you don't
have to put people on hold,

00:15:27.800 --> 00:15:30.900
and search, and ask around
for different answers.

00:15:30.900 --> 00:15:36.290
And really makes the
new agent more aware

00:15:36.290 --> 00:15:40.250
of the brand taxonomy, and
whatever terms the company is

00:15:40.250 --> 00:15:42.590
using, and types of
answers the company

00:15:42.590 --> 00:15:44.840
wants the agent to provide
to their customers.

00:15:44.840 --> 00:15:47.710
So lot of benefits.

00:15:47.710 --> 00:15:50.320
Agent Assist was
launched last year

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:53.070
as a voice channel
transcribing all calls,

00:15:53.070 --> 00:15:55.960
and we've had a lot of customer
asking for that same experience

00:15:55.960 --> 00:15:57.370
on chat.

00:15:57.370 --> 00:16:00.160
So I'm announcing today
a second announcement,

00:16:00.160 --> 00:16:02.130
Agent Assist for Chat.

00:16:02.130 --> 00:16:05.470
Agent Assist for Chat does
all the same thing as voice.

00:16:05.470 --> 00:16:08.035
Will do document suggestion
if it gets [INAUDIBLE],,

00:16:08.035 --> 00:16:10.000
but because you're
on a chat channel

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:12.470
you can do a little bit more.

00:16:12.470 --> 00:16:16.210
So you can do smart
replies and smart compose.

00:16:16.210 --> 00:16:19.360
Smart replies are proposing
several possible replies

00:16:19.360 --> 00:16:22.450
that the agent can
click, modify, and send

00:16:22.450 --> 00:16:24.920
right away to the customer.

00:16:24.920 --> 00:16:26.590
This is very
helpful in a context

00:16:26.590 --> 00:16:30.340
where agents have two or
three chats in parallel,

00:16:30.340 --> 00:16:32.763
and they have to switch
context all the time.

00:16:32.763 --> 00:16:34.930
Because of the smart reply,
they can go to a window,

00:16:34.930 --> 00:16:37.910
quickly see what kind of replies
they can send, and send them.

00:16:37.910 --> 00:16:39.370
Shorter time again.

00:16:39.370 --> 00:16:41.715
More accurate responses again.

00:16:41.715 --> 00:16:43.090
Smart compose is
what you've been

00:16:43.090 --> 00:16:46.930
used to seeing in Gmail,
for example, or your G Docs.

00:16:46.930 --> 00:16:50.200
If you're typing, it
types ahead, saving time,

00:16:50.200 --> 00:16:53.260
reducing the number of typos,
having more accurate responses.

00:16:53.260 --> 00:16:55.150
We integrated the
same technology

00:16:55.150 --> 00:16:58.600
Gmail uses in the chat window.

00:16:58.600 --> 00:17:03.040
Again, efficiency for agents,
better user experience,

00:17:03.040 --> 00:17:06.400
less training required, and
lower onboarding effort.

00:17:06.400 --> 00:17:07.300
A great launch.

00:17:07.300 --> 00:17:08.890
Very excited.

00:17:08.890 --> 00:17:12.890
This is really interesting.

00:17:12.890 --> 00:17:16.630
Let's look at the third pillar,
CCAI Insights, in alpha right

00:17:16.630 --> 00:17:18.119
now.

00:17:18.119 --> 00:17:20.520
Insights is about
visualizing and understanding

00:17:20.520 --> 00:17:24.300
what is going on in context in
your contact center operations.

00:17:24.300 --> 00:17:27.000
From pure data,
just how many calls,

00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:29.670
and your lengths of
calls, but really drilling

00:17:29.670 --> 00:17:32.070
in the voice conversation.

00:17:32.070 --> 00:17:33.750
That's important
because, as I said,

00:17:33.750 --> 00:17:35.950
you probably do record
your conversation.

00:17:35.950 --> 00:17:37.530
But do you know
what's being said

00:17:37.530 --> 00:17:39.120
without doing just Sampling?

00:17:39.120 --> 00:17:41.810
Sampling will make you
miss a lot of things.

00:17:41.810 --> 00:17:44.520
A lot of intents are not
detected about subjects,

00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:46.980
a lot of topics are
not found, especially

00:17:46.980 --> 00:17:49.020
if they are not that common.

00:17:49.020 --> 00:17:51.840
With Insights, you
can have topic modeler

00:17:51.840 --> 00:17:54.690
and really understand all
the drivers of your calls,

00:17:54.690 --> 00:17:57.750
but go to a conversation
level and really

00:17:57.750 --> 00:18:00.450
see what conversations
happen, what

00:18:00.450 --> 00:18:02.460
are the sentiment
across conversation,

00:18:02.460 --> 00:18:05.940
drill in a conversation,
see all the transcriptions,

00:18:05.940 --> 00:18:08.610
hear the audio if you're
authorized to do so,

00:18:08.610 --> 00:18:10.540
and really understand
what's going on.

00:18:10.540 --> 00:18:14.850
You can do compliance check,
and QA managers in particular

00:18:14.850 --> 00:18:16.718
love this product
because you can

00:18:16.718 --> 00:18:19.260
search through the conversation
and find all the conversation

00:18:19.260 --> 00:18:21.860
that were not good that they
can really focus instead

00:18:21.860 --> 00:18:24.360
of doing sampling and trying
to find out which ones were not

00:18:24.360 --> 00:18:25.270
good.

00:18:25.270 --> 00:18:27.130
It's very hard to do
on [INAUDIBLE] only.

00:18:27.130 --> 00:18:30.080
With insights, it's trivial.

00:18:30.080 --> 00:18:32.130
We're really excited
about CCAI Insights.

00:18:32.130 --> 00:18:34.470
Still in alpha right
now, but very quickly

00:18:34.470 --> 00:18:36.095
will go to into beta.

00:18:36.095 --> 00:18:37.970
It does conversational
analytics, [INAUDIBLE]

00:18:37.970 --> 00:18:39.770
session reviews, as I said.

00:18:39.770 --> 00:18:42.390
It's a complete
solution that integrates

00:18:42.390 --> 00:18:44.460
with the rest of the
Google Cloud Platform.

00:18:44.460 --> 00:18:47.515
For example, a native
export to BigQuery.

00:18:47.515 --> 00:18:48.140
Very important.

00:18:48.140 --> 00:18:50.960
You have CRM data, or ERP
data, or other database

00:18:50.960 --> 00:18:52.980
that you want to
ingest into BigQuery

00:18:52.980 --> 00:18:56.130
and map it to your customer
interactions in your contact

00:18:56.130 --> 00:18:57.090
center.

00:18:57.090 --> 00:18:59.010
Insights allows you to do that.

00:18:59.010 --> 00:19:01.260
Very easy to tie
all that information

00:19:01.260 --> 00:19:04.177
from your customer
interaction into the rest

00:19:04.177 --> 00:19:05.760
of the interaction
with your customer,

00:19:05.760 --> 00:19:07.510
either your marketing
campaign, your sales

00:19:07.510 --> 00:19:10.910
campaign, or whatever
interactions you have.

00:19:10.910 --> 00:19:14.620
This allows you to really
leverage the power of Google

00:19:14.620 --> 00:19:17.750
and the power of
the platform itself.

00:19:17.750 --> 00:19:19.445
And finally, that
search and query

00:19:19.445 --> 00:19:21.820
allows you to find information
within those conversations

00:19:21.820 --> 00:19:24.700
very quickly and really
focus your effort

00:19:24.700 --> 00:19:28.100
to improve your
customer experience.

00:19:28.100 --> 00:19:29.750
So if we dive a
little bit deeper

00:19:29.750 --> 00:19:35.040
into each of the underlying
technology for those services,

00:19:35.040 --> 00:19:37.050
the goal of Google AI
is really to enable

00:19:37.050 --> 00:19:40.870
conversations that are as
close to human as possible.

00:19:40.870 --> 00:19:43.230
We're not claiming that we're
going to replace humans.

00:19:43.230 --> 00:19:44.680
That's not the case.

00:19:44.680 --> 00:19:46.680
But we want to be close
enough, and actually, we

00:19:46.680 --> 00:19:48.660
advise our customers--

00:19:48.660 --> 00:19:50.970
because you'll hear some
of the custom voice thing--

00:19:50.970 --> 00:19:52.080
we're going to
advise our customer

00:19:52.080 --> 00:19:53.705
to let people know
that they're talking

00:19:53.705 --> 00:19:55.740
to a bot and not a human agent.

00:19:55.740 --> 00:19:58.320
That trust is very
important, and we

00:19:58.320 --> 00:20:01.540
want to make sure that
consumers and customers are not

00:20:01.540 --> 00:20:04.770
deceived by the experience
we're providing with Google AI.

00:20:04.770 --> 00:20:09.010
Google AI benefits from deep
research in our Google AI team,

00:20:09.010 --> 00:20:10.710
but DeepMind as well.

00:20:10.710 --> 00:20:14.860
But benefits also from a
lot of the Google search

00:20:14.860 --> 00:20:16.870
research that is happening.

00:20:16.870 --> 00:20:21.310
Lots of very, very
smart AI people

00:20:21.310 --> 00:20:24.700
working on search, voice
search, working on the Google

00:20:24.700 --> 00:20:26.770
Assistant, working
on the Google Home,

00:20:26.770 --> 00:20:30.380
even building specialized
hardware for AI-- our TPU

00:20:30.380 --> 00:20:33.520
that you may hear
in other sessions,

00:20:33.520 --> 00:20:37.720
our TPU allows us to iterate on
the models and the performance

00:20:37.720 --> 00:20:40.960
of those models and do
inference very, very fast.

00:20:40.960 --> 00:20:43.030
Enabling, for
example, the speech

00:20:43.030 --> 00:20:46.430
to text in real time
in streaming mode.

00:20:46.430 --> 00:20:48.370
So with those
technologies underneath,

00:20:48.370 --> 00:20:50.710
we can create AI that
understand what's going on,

00:20:50.710 --> 00:20:55.020
that can talk and interact
with the customers.

00:20:55.020 --> 00:20:58.100
If you think about speech
to text in particular,

00:20:58.100 --> 00:21:01.660
a core underlying technology
for conversational AI.

00:21:01.660 --> 00:21:03.180
Massive dictionary.

00:21:03.180 --> 00:21:07.060
120 languages
supported in the API.

00:21:07.060 --> 00:21:10.390
You can bias the
model at each turn.

00:21:10.390 --> 00:21:11.830
And actually that
biasing is used

00:21:11.830 --> 00:21:16.330
in Dialogflow which, just
by clicking one checkbox,

00:21:16.330 --> 00:21:19.165
can bias the model in each
turn of the conversation

00:21:19.165 --> 00:21:20.120
inside Dialogflow.

00:21:20.120 --> 00:21:22.130
It's called [INAUDIBLE]
speech biasing.

00:21:22.130 --> 00:21:24.490
Dialogflow knows the context.

00:21:24.490 --> 00:21:27.610
Speech to text can be biased
based on that context,

00:21:27.610 --> 00:21:29.740
and automatically
that biasing's done,

00:21:29.740 --> 00:21:32.702
which makes the intent
detection accuracy much higher

00:21:32.702 --> 00:21:34.160
than if you don't
use that biasing.

00:21:34.160 --> 00:21:36.400
And that's currently
unique to Google.

00:21:36.400 --> 00:21:41.240
We can detect several languages,
formatting, do real time.

00:21:41.240 --> 00:21:44.690
I'll spend a little bit of
time on the language support.

00:21:44.690 --> 00:21:47.780
Many companies will
claim language support.

00:21:47.780 --> 00:21:50.360
Many companies will claim that
their model works best here

00:21:50.360 --> 00:21:51.250
and there.

00:21:51.250 --> 00:21:55.070
You have to be careful about
models in speech to text.

00:21:55.070 --> 00:21:57.020
You will need
different models based

00:21:57.020 --> 00:22:00.440
on the quality of the audio,
based on who's on the line,

00:22:00.440 --> 00:22:02.270
based on the length, et cetera.

00:22:02.270 --> 00:22:07.570
Let's do a speech to text
for a long conversation,

00:22:07.570 --> 00:22:10.460
a video, subtitle.

00:22:10.460 --> 00:22:14.180
It's a very different audio
quality really from your noise

00:22:14.180 --> 00:22:16.922
background than an agent
in a contact center.

00:22:16.922 --> 00:22:18.380
If you've been in
a contact center,

00:22:18.380 --> 00:22:20.780
you know it's a very
noisy environment.

00:22:20.780 --> 00:22:21.590
A little headset.

00:22:21.590 --> 00:22:24.330
People are talking
left and right.

00:22:24.330 --> 00:22:27.650
It's hard, and most people
will call from a phone, eight

00:22:27.650 --> 00:22:29.330
kilohertz quality.

00:22:29.330 --> 00:22:31.610
Two people are
talking on the line.

00:22:31.610 --> 00:22:32.570
Very difficult.

00:22:32.570 --> 00:22:35.060
If you apply the
same speech model

00:22:35.060 --> 00:22:38.840
from transcribing
a video conference

00:22:38.840 --> 00:22:42.450
in a context of contact center,
that's not going to work.

00:22:42.450 --> 00:22:46.070
We have five languages
optimized for telephony,

00:22:46.070 --> 00:22:48.080
optimized for contact center.

00:22:48.080 --> 00:22:52.100
Spanish, English-- English
US, and English UK--

00:22:52.100 --> 00:22:55.440
Russian, and I think we have
another one I can't remember.

00:22:55.440 --> 00:22:59.570
Australian or
something like that.

00:22:59.570 --> 00:23:02.270
Those language supports are very
important because they really

00:23:02.270 --> 00:23:04.580
make sure that the transcription
are done correctly,

00:23:04.580 --> 00:23:06.663
that the documents suggestions
are done correctly,

00:23:06.663 --> 00:23:09.770
and then the insights we get
out of it are done correctly.

00:23:09.770 --> 00:23:13.700
Very important to think about
the optimisation of the models

00:23:13.700 --> 00:23:16.820
that you have and
what we can provide

00:23:16.820 --> 00:23:18.050
based on what use cases.

00:23:18.050 --> 00:23:21.830
The IVR model will be different
from the Agent Assist model,

00:23:21.830 --> 00:23:25.250
and we provide all of those.

00:23:25.250 --> 00:23:28.380
In speech to text, innovation
is happening almost

00:23:28.380 --> 00:23:29.610
on a monthly basis.

00:23:29.610 --> 00:23:31.845
Pretty impressive improvements.

00:23:31.845 --> 00:23:35.520
But today, we're announcing
the third big move

00:23:35.520 --> 00:23:40.460
in conversational AI,
speech to text on-prem.

00:23:40.460 --> 00:23:43.110
Speech to text on-prem was
a big ask from customers,

00:23:43.110 --> 00:23:47.830
especially in the financial
services and health care.

00:23:47.830 --> 00:23:51.240
What they really want, that
processing of the raw voice

00:23:51.240 --> 00:23:54.510
data done on premise
before it hits the cloud

00:23:54.510 --> 00:23:56.530
for other types of processing.

00:23:56.530 --> 00:23:59.850
For example, a DLP processing--
the data loss prevention.

00:23:59.850 --> 00:24:03.540
Removing all the
PIs from the text.

00:24:03.540 --> 00:24:07.050
Now you can deploy speech to
text on-prem using Anthos,

00:24:07.050 --> 00:24:09.290
which is our platform
for their multi cloud

00:24:09.290 --> 00:24:10.970
and hybrid platform.

00:24:10.970 --> 00:24:13.400
You can deploy speech
to text on-prem

00:24:13.400 --> 00:24:15.300
and have exactly the
same functionality you

00:24:15.300 --> 00:24:17.790
would get under the cloud API.

00:24:17.790 --> 00:24:20.700
The US English
support, multi-channel

00:24:20.700 --> 00:24:26.130
with several people talking,
recognition, the diarization,

00:24:26.130 --> 00:24:28.020
custom recognition
of specific words,

00:24:28.020 --> 00:24:30.240
very important for health
care and financial services

00:24:30.240 --> 00:24:31.090
as well, actually.

00:24:31.090 --> 00:24:34.290
A lot of terms are specific
to those industries.

00:24:34.290 --> 00:24:36.690
And the noise robustness
that is required

00:24:36.690 --> 00:24:39.000
in particular in contact
center environment

00:24:39.000 --> 00:24:42.330
when there's a lot of
background noise, et cetera.

00:24:42.330 --> 00:24:44.640
So this is a super
exciting launch.

00:24:44.640 --> 00:24:48.630
Managing the on-prem deployment
through an Anthos console

00:24:48.630 --> 00:24:49.800
in the cloud.

00:24:49.800 --> 00:24:53.790
So doing the on-prem the cloud
way with the modern deployment

00:24:53.790 --> 00:24:56.742
capabilities, the
tracking, the analytics,

00:24:56.742 --> 00:24:58.200
and all the good
things you get out

00:24:58.200 --> 00:25:01.530
of Anthos now enabling
speech to text

00:25:01.530 --> 00:25:03.628
to be available for you on-prem.

00:25:03.628 --> 00:25:04.920
Very excited about this launch.

00:25:08.040 --> 00:25:10.750
Now let's talk about the other
way around, when you have text

00:25:10.750 --> 00:25:12.180
and you want to speak it out.

00:25:12.180 --> 00:25:14.370
We have been leading
at Google text

00:25:14.370 --> 00:25:18.430
to speech with several
generation of technologies.

00:25:18.430 --> 00:25:21.780
The first one that's being
used right now quite a lot

00:25:21.780 --> 00:25:22.920
is WaveNet.

00:25:22.920 --> 00:25:27.600
WaveNet came from our research
arm at Google DeepMinds,

00:25:27.600 --> 00:25:31.920
and it's a voice that
sounds fairly engaging.

00:25:31.920 --> 00:25:33.810
Pretty close to a human voice.

00:25:33.810 --> 00:25:36.930
Much less robotic than
the traditional voice.

00:25:36.930 --> 00:25:39.390
You have 220 voices
to choose from--

00:25:39.390 --> 00:25:44.670
female, male, different tones
and speed, and 92 of them

00:25:44.670 --> 00:25:48.060
are WaveNet voices
across 32 languages.

00:25:48.060 --> 00:25:50.010
It is actually very
easy to go test it out.

00:25:50.010 --> 00:25:52.940
You go on
cloud.google.com/texttospeech.

00:25:52.940 --> 00:25:55.020
You can put a little
text, choose your voice,

00:25:55.020 --> 00:25:58.950
choose your language, and
you will hear it speak.

00:25:58.950 --> 00:26:00.240
Very easy.

00:26:00.240 --> 00:26:02.320
I actually have a little
bit of fun myself.

00:26:02.320 --> 00:26:04.537
I put, for example, English
and I take the French--

00:26:04.537 --> 00:26:06.120
I put some French
in there, and I play

00:26:06.120 --> 00:26:08.630
with the little combination.

00:26:08.630 --> 00:26:10.380
It's very interesting
to see what it does,

00:26:10.380 --> 00:26:12.540
but it's pretty
impressive, actually.

00:26:12.540 --> 00:26:15.132
So WaveNet has been
a great success.

00:26:15.132 --> 00:26:16.590
The text to speech
API is very easy

00:26:16.590 --> 00:26:18.450
to use through REST or GRPC.

00:26:18.450 --> 00:26:22.140
You can insert pronunciation
hints, like pauses

00:26:22.140 --> 00:26:23.737
and how to say certain numbers.

00:26:23.737 --> 00:26:26.070
Obviously you're not going
to say a phone number the way

00:26:26.070 --> 00:26:31.080
you say a dollar amount, or a
credit card number, or a date.

00:26:31.080 --> 00:26:34.710
So all those you can put little
hints in the text for the text

00:26:34.710 --> 00:26:39.480
to speech API to adapt
to what needs to be said.

00:26:39.480 --> 00:26:42.480
One thing we're announcing
today, I talked about WaveNet

00:26:42.480 --> 00:26:44.370
and how it works great.

00:26:44.370 --> 00:26:47.560
We have now custom
speech, custom voice.

00:26:47.560 --> 00:26:49.590
It's basically the
ability to train

00:26:49.590 --> 00:26:55.710
a specific model for a customer
based on a human voice actor

00:26:55.710 --> 00:27:00.870
and replicate that voice actor's
voice for text to speech.

00:27:00.870 --> 00:27:05.310
I can already hear some of
you say, oh, that's scary.

00:27:05.310 --> 00:27:07.320
Well, the technology
is pretty impressive,

00:27:07.320 --> 00:27:09.090
and you'll hear about it.

00:27:09.090 --> 00:27:13.260
But we require an ethics review
for any deployment and access

00:27:13.260 --> 00:27:14.532
to this API.

00:27:14.532 --> 00:27:15.990
We want to make
sure that customers

00:27:15.990 --> 00:27:18.520
are using custom voice
in an ethical way

00:27:18.520 --> 00:27:21.480
and are not abusing
the voice of an actor

00:27:21.480 --> 00:27:23.960
to do things that
are not ethical.

00:27:23.960 --> 00:27:28.442
So we are gating access to
this API through a review.

00:27:28.442 --> 00:27:32.150
And when you hear the example,
I think you'll understand why.

00:27:32.150 --> 00:27:35.140
So let's listen to
two simple audio.

00:27:35.140 --> 00:27:38.260
One is a human actor
saying a sentence,

00:27:38.260 --> 00:27:40.420
and the other one
is the model based

00:27:40.420 --> 00:27:43.780
on that human actor saying a
sentence that this human actor

00:27:43.780 --> 00:27:45.100
never say.

00:27:45.100 --> 00:27:48.386
Let's listen to the
first one, the actor.

00:27:48.386 --> 00:27:49.670
[AUDIO PLAYBACK]

00:27:49.670 --> 00:27:52.220
- According to Science
Direct, a flexed posture

00:27:52.220 --> 00:27:55.040
is characterized by an
increased thoracic kyphosis.

00:27:55.040 --> 00:27:56.180
[END PLAYBACK]

00:27:56.180 --> 00:27:57.440
ANTONY PASSEMARD:
Obviously I'm not a doctor,

00:27:57.440 --> 00:27:58.860
and I have no idea
what she said.

00:27:58.860 --> 00:28:02.390
But it's interesting to hear
her voice and her pitch.

00:28:02.390 --> 00:28:05.713
So now let's listen to the
bot version of that voice.

00:28:05.713 --> 00:28:06.380
